n{{field-2}}.  \n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, our Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2t) \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }t\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-333863/field-1" "q-333863/field-2" "q-333863/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee461d3e-3afa-51c6-bddd-355668cac3ec"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-333863"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-333869/field-1/24f5d131e97c1627fc29aa2ae02206f640cc05051a9d0015ebf90a30c2452c14" :answer/id #uuid "b2cce395-3468-5d5c-a62d-5e15e1ca8674" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/ensure [:answer/valid