f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-333869/field-2/aa8b3f9c78bb7b825079ad4aaf338d07c96e446b686a39a5121216be4228efdb" :answer-field/choices ["q-333869/field-2/b458e7ce396e2645405ae9ea0b183f63221f0e5b935ce41459202d3714e3b25a" "q-333869/field-2/aa8b3f9c78bb7b825079ad4aaf338d07c96e446b686a39a5121216be4228efdb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333869/field-3/a8a725fec0b63a3b8d6f4591903bfe81596da23bd6deceff5c00414df35bbd3f" :answer/id #uuid "92e93494-fd2e-58fb-81aa-7bcd14f3687a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "independent" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333869/field-3/988842f9ae7a51d1679b23c13893e9f8d3070ea423527b72c2d8836642aabb0a" :answer/id #uuid "2c7e17b4-10f8-5c6b-b998-51987334ec32" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "dependent" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333869/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "30e91b8e-e59f-5b07-aa2f-42ae582af684" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-333869/field-3/a8a725fec0b63a3b8d6f4591903bfe81596da23bd6deceff5c00414df35bbd3f" :answer-field/choices ["q-333869/field-3/a8a725fec0b63a3b8d6f4591903bfe81596da23bd6deceff5c00414df35bbd3f" "q-333869/field-3/988842f9ae7a51d1679b23c13893e9f8d3070ea423527b72c2d8836642aabb0a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333869" :question/id #uuid "5fb9112c-327a-5fd3-bfe1-5d6b3ff9c5b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-333869" :question/problem "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the vector-valued functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ above. Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions of the same system of linear ODEs, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},\n\n$\\,$ which is\n{{field-2}}.  \n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, our Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡t+9{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡t+9{sin}^{2}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }t\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-333869/field-1" "q-333869/field-2" "q-333869/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee461d3e-3afa-51c6-bddd-355668cac3ec"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-333869"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-334404/field-1/61af73b8e8ead9835a9b9a8e325b35ee392bbd1ef8d56b833ee0389146eb9f6a" :answer/id #uuid "e2513fe3-9e91-506d-a04c-81b03761b53e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "isn't" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334404/field-1/