53e1f10-2890-53a0-8edb-393837e733c1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334404/field-6" :answer-field/id #uuid "9e456b64-95df-5f90-bafd-8a489dfdf098" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-334404/field-6/e83e88c35e8a3ffe3d8d80688728f9214cfbf65c3093a19a019d6894ed2a6d30" :answer-field/choices ["q-334404/field-6/fe99e0aa6d4cb233aa2e049abbad820f870e86f699ff77516dd4d190bd7c0759" "q-334404/field-6/e83e88c35e8a3ffe3d8d80688728f9214cfbf65c3093a19a019d6894ed2a6d30" "q-334404/field-6/85c7c9ebbc82f2132462de6e483703806c7dbaa0850f97a340e2063a2fd7d348" "q-334404/field-6/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334404" :question/id #uuid "d134dbd9-abad-5169-a7f6-3f9e65bfe7ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-334404" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs above, where $t\\in R.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The function $a(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ \n{{field-1}} \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ The function $b(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 2{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}]$ \n{{field-2}} \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ The function $c(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]$ \n{{field-3}} \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ {{field-4}} \nis a set of fundamental solutions to the system because the Wronskian \n\n$$\nW({{field-5}})={{field-6}}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n$\\,$ for at least one $t\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nFirst, we write our system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute the given vectors into the matrix differential equation:\n\n- $a(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(4{e}^{2t})^{′} \\\\ (0)^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}8{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot 4{e}^{2t}+1\\cdot 0 \\\\ 0\\cdot 4{e}^{2t}+3\\cdot 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}8{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}8{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $b(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 2{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}]$ $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(2{e}^{3t})^{′} \\\\ (2{e}^{3t})^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 2{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6{e}^{3t} \\\\ 6{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot 2{e}^{3t}+1\\cdot 2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0\\cdot 2{e}^{3t}+3\\cdot 2{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6{e}^{3t} \\\\ 6{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6{e}^{3t} \\\\ 6{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $c(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]$ $\\text{isn't}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}({e}^{2t})^{′} \\\\ ({e}^{2t})^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{2t} \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot {e}^{2t}+1\\cdot {e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\cdot {e}^{2t}+3\\cdot {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{2t} \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{2t} \\\\ 3{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, consider the set of solutions ${a,b}.$ Its Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(a,b) & =|\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{2t} & 2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & 2{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(4{e}^{2t})\\cdot 2{e}^{3t}-(2{e}^{3t})\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =8{e}^{5t}-0 \\\\  & =8{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is nonzero for at least one $t\\in R.$ Thus, since $a$ and $b$ are both solutions of the same linear system, and their Wronskian is nonzero for at least one $t\\in R,$ ${a,b}$ is a set of fundamental solutions of the system." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-334404/field-1" "q-334404/field-2" "q-334404/field-3" "q-334404/field-4" "q-334404/field-5" "q-334404/field-6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac674e3f-ac2b-5512-b4fa-9840c6cbd034"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334404"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
